  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Active pattern starting late tonight through \nmidweek.\n\nA more active pattern will begin late tonight as high pressure \nover the region drifts east, allowing a warm frontal segment to \ndrift north in response to a mid/upper shortwave dropping into \nthe Upper Midwest. This will bring the next chance for showers \nand storms into western New York well after midnight tonight, \nshifting east across the rest of the area through early Monday \nafternoon. \n\nAs the system moves northeast later in the day, a trailing cold\nfront will push south across western and north-central New York \nMonday afternoon and evening. A moist airmass will be in place \nwithin the warm sector ahead of the boundary, with PWAT values \nin the 1.5\"-1.75\" range. This will contribute to at least \nmoderate diurnal instability to aid the development of scattered\nafternoon and evening thunderstorms. \n\nThere will be the potential for locally heavy rainfall within the \nmoist airmass, for which WPC has a Marginal Risk for Excessive \nRainfall (Level 1 out of 4). There will also be the threat of severe \nweather, mainly across the Southern Tier where moderate shear \nprofiles will be in place, however instability may not be sufficient \nto sustain more robust convection. Nonetheless, potential for a \nstronger storm or two will exist, with the main threat being strong \nwinds. SPC maintaining the Marginal Risk of Severe Weather (Level 1 \nout of 5) across the Southern Tier. The greater severe risk remains \nto our southwest at this time.\n\nThe cold front will stall just to our south Monday night, then \nremain nearly stationary through midweek. Drier air will reside to \nthe north of the boundary Tuesday, with the better threat of showers \nand storms across the western Southern Tier closer to the frontal \nboundary. A potent mid level shortwave and associated surface low \nwill cross the lower Great Lakes Wednesday, bringing a renewed \nthreat of showers and storms by midweek.",
